              Not everything in CMDS is a command              ===================================925 of the 949 files under /dd/CMDS are ordinary 68k program modules.  Theother 24 are not, and running one of them "to see if it works" proves nothingeither way.  Here is what they are.  `tools/module_census.py` in the sourcerepository regenerates this list by reading M$Type and M$Lang out of everyheader.TRAP LIBRARIES -- type $0B.  A program links these; you do not run them.    cio         Microware's C library for cc 3.2 and earlier, and what the    math        star in DOC/INDEX marks.  math is single precision;    math881     math881 uses a 68881 coprocessor.  Shipped by permission.    csl         Ultra C's C Shared Library, and the 68020/030/040 build of    csl020      it.  Shipped by permission.    X11R6shl    X11R6 shared library loader    graph       the Atari `Graph' library.  g, striche, apfel, sine,                showpic, graphdemo and graphsave all link it.  It was                catalogued as a graphics demo for years; it is a library.SYSTEM MODULES AND DRIVERS -- types $0C and $0E.  These belong in a system'smodule directory, not on a command line.  All seven are MM/1 hardware.    keydrv.mm1        keyboard driver          (module name: keydrv)    msdrv.901_340     mouse driver             (module name: msdrv)    msdrv_340.901.ms  mouse driver, variant    (module name: msdrv)    snddrv            sound driver    rb37c65           floppy driver, 37C65 controller    windio.52         windowing terminal driver (module name: windio)    scsi_mm1a         SCSI subroutine moduleBASIC09 I-CODE -- type $02, language $02.  Not 68000 code at all: compiledBASIC09 I-code, which needs Microware's `runb` to run.  Nothing on this diskcan run them, and no amount of cio changes that.  They were once recorded hereas failing with an illegal instruction, which is true but beside the point.    bio         biorhythm chart (F. Kaefer 1987)    wysetime    Wyse terminal time utility    blackjack   Las Vegas blackjack (M. Theys) -- three modules in one file:                blackjack, draw and shuffle    dedit       disk sector editor (Carl Kreider) -- NINE modules in the one                file: dedit, hex_dec, dec_hex, get_media_size, p_sector,                go_xy, p_sir, e_sector, rd_sectorThe way to tell one of these from a broken 68000 program is to start it:OS-9 answers E_BMID (205), `bad module ID', because it was asked to forkI-code as machine code.  That message reads like damage and is not.  It ishow `dedit' came to be excluded from this collection once, and how `bio'nearly was.  Compare the header bytes against `bio' before believing aprogram is broken: an I-code module has $00 where a 68000 program has $01.DATA MODULES -- type $04.    rtfdat      RTF FORTRAN data module    wn.stb      WN's symbol tableSHELL SCRIPTS -- not modules at all.    keep        take programs from this disk onto your own /dd    drop        put them back, exactly, using keep's receipt    kept        list what keep has installed    who         who is logged in -- Microware shell syntax    mscheck     mtools disk verifier -- a ksh scriptMODULE GROUPS -- one file, several modules.  OS-9 loads the whole group andkeeps it resident until the combined link count reaches zero, so the extramodules are not leftovers.    lfmaker     lfmaker (program) + lfmaker_win (window descriptor)    cyberwar    CyberWar and cyber_game (both programs) + cyber_start_win                and cyber_retry_win (window descriptors)    blackjack   blackjack, draw and shuffle (all BASIC09 I-code)Seven more files are a byte or two longer than their module -- trailingpadding, not a second module.  Count $4AFC headers, not bytes, before callinganything a group.ONE MORE THING WORTH KNOWING65 files report a module name that is not their filename.  Most aredeliberate: `cjpeg.070`, `math881`, `gzip68k_csl` and the rest of theCMDS/REBUILT variants are alternates kept side by side under distinctfilenames.  Seven are NOT deliberate, and are a build mistake this collectionmade itself -- a missing `-n=<name>` at link time, so the module took its namefrom the output file of the moment:    compress -> R_compress      queens -> B_baruch    kermit   -> R_kermit        tabs   -> B_tabs    screen   -> R_screen        hc     -> B_hc                                wish   -> B_wishThey run.  They just report the wrong name in their own usage text and in amodule directory listing.